<<<
+ letter from Sir Antony MacDonnell
===
+ speech has drawn an interesting letter from Sir Antony MacDonnell
>>>

<<<
was too weak either to secure the dismissal
===
was too weak *either to secure* the dismissal
>>>

<<<
stated by its author:—“ Its main idea is fo historically
===
stated by its author:—“ Its main idea is *to* historically
>>>

<<<
divinely—from the Divine point of view—impeach the European system
===
divinely—from the Divine point of view—*impeach* the European system
>>>

<<<
of Church & States ’’.’.
===
of Church & States ".'.
>>>

<<<
SPLIT VERBS. There can be little doubt that the position of his troops all the way from Berat northward will seriously be imperilled. For questions such as
===
**SPLIT VERBS.** *There can be little doubt that the position of his troops all the way from Berat northward will seriously be imperilled.* For questions such as
>>>

<<<
last four words of this, see POSITION OF ADVERBS, 4.
===
last four words of this, see ^^POSITION OF ADVERBS^^, 4.
>>>

<<<
splodge, splotch. The second is
===
**splodge, splotch.** The second is
>>>

<<<
more descriptive; cf. SLUSH, & SMUDGE.
===
more descriptive; cf. ^^SLUSH^^, & ^^SMUDGE^^.
>>>

<<<
splutter, sputter. Without any
===
**splutter, sputter.** Without any
>>>

<<<
it may be said that in sputter the notion
===
it may be said that in *sputter* the notion
>>>

<<<
spoil. For spoiled, -lt, see -T & -ED.
===
**spoil.** For *spoiled, -lt,* see ^^-T^^ & ^^-ED^^.
>>>

<<<
-spoken. For the curious use in fair, free, soft, out, &c., -s (where soft-speeched &c. might have been expected), see INTRANSITIVE P.P.
===
**-spoken.** For the curious use in *fair, free, soft, out,* &c., *-s* (where *soft-speeched* &c. might have been expected), see ^^INTRANSITIVE P.P.^^
>>>

<<<
these compounds fair- &c. are adverbial as much as out-, & that
===
these compounds *fair-* &c. are adverbial as much as *out-*, & that
>>>

<<<
sponge makes spongeable, see -ABLE 1; but sponging & spongy, see MUTE E.
===
**spondee.** See ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^.

**sponge** makes *spongeable*, see ^^-ABLE^^ 1; but *sponging* & *spongy*, see ^^MUTE E^^.
>>>

<<<
spontaneity,-ousness. -TY & -NESS.
===
**spontaneity, -ousness.** ^^-TY^^ & ^^-NESS^^.
>>>

<<<
spook. Pronounce -ōök.
===
**spook.** Pronounce -o͞ok.
>>>

<<<
spoondrift. See SPINDRIFT.
===
**spoondrift.** See ^^SPINDRIFT^^.
>>>

<<<
spoon(e)y. The adjective should be -ny, see -EX & -Y; for the noun, in which either is legitimate, & -ey probably more frequent, -EY, -IE, -Y.
===
**spoon(e)y.** The adjective should be *-ny*, see ^^-EY^^ & ^^-Y^^; for the noun, in which either is legitimate, & *-ey* probably more frequent, ^^-EY^^, ^^-IE^^, ^^-Y^^.
>>>

<<<
spoonful. Pl. spoonfuls; see -FUL.
===
**spoonful.** Pl. *spoonfuls*; see ^^-FUL^^.
>>>

<<<
spouse. For the use in ordinary writing in preference to wife, see FORMAL WORDS; but s. is serviceable
===
**spouse.** For the use in ordinary writing in preference to *wife*, see ^^FORMAL WORDS^^; but *s.* is serviceable
>>>

<<<
sprain)(strain. It is natural
===
**sprain)(strain.** It is natural
>>>

<<<
but even in medical books they are often treated as equivalent. Sprain, perhaps, describes the result rather of a momentary wrench or twist, & strain that of an exertion of muscle too strong or too long foritscapacity.
===
but even in medical books they are often treated as equivalent. *Sprain*, perhaps, describes the result rather of a momentary wrench or twist, & *strain* that of an exertion of muscle too strong or too long for its capacity.
>>>

<<<
spray, nn., make sprayey; see -EY & -y, exception 1.
===
**spray, nn.,** make *sprayey*; see ^^-EY^^ & ^^-Y^^, exception 1.
>>>

<<<
spray, vb. Forinflexions see VERBS IN -IE &e., 1.
===
**spray, vb.** For inflexions see ^^VERBS IN -IE^^ &c., 1.
>>>

<<<
spring. The past sprang is considerably more frequent than sprung, both
===
**spring.** The past *sprang* is considerably more frequent than *sprung*, both
>>>

<<<
spring, n. The compounds, like those of srEA, are of interest to
===
**spring, n.** The compounds, like those of ^^SEA^^, are of interest to
>>>

<<<
For the principle, see HyPHENS 3 B; spring-bed, spring - mattress, spring-gun, are usually forbidden by the accent, & must be changed to two words each;
===
For the principle, see ^^HYPHENS^^ 3 B; *spring-bed, spring-mattress, spring-gun,* are usually forbidden by the accent, & must be changed to two words each;
>>>

<<<
spring -time & spring-board are allowed by accent, unless springtime & springboard are preferred; springtide or springtide can stand only for the season,
===
*spring-time* & *spring-board* are allowed by accent, unless *springtime* & *springboard* are preferred; *spring-tide* or *springtide* can stand only for the season,
>>>

<<<
& the tidal term must be spring tide in two words; spring(-) cart will usually be two words, but may be hyphened when all the stress is on spring & a spring-cart is to be distinguished
===
& the tidal term must be *spring tide* in two words; *spring(-)cart* will usually be two words, but may be hyphened when all the stress is on *spring* & a spring-cart is to be distinguished
>>>
